.TH C5XLNK 1 "2026-10-05" "c5xtools 1.0.1" "c5xtools Manual"
.SH NAME
c5xlnk \- COFF linker for the TMS320C5x fixed-point DSP
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B c5xlnk
.IR object .obj ...
.RI [ cmdfile ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-m
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-e
.IR symbol ]
.RB [ \-l
.IR lib ]
.RB [ \-i
.IR dir ]
.RB [ \-u
.IR symbol ]
.RB [ \-x ]
.RB [ \-r | \-ar ]
.RB [ \-s ]
.RB [ \-\-static ]
.RB [ \-b ]
.RB [ \-v0 ]
.RB [ \-flat ]
.RB [ \-text
.IR addr ]
.RB [ \-data
.IR addr ]
.RB [ \-bss
.IR addr ]
.RB [ \-V | \-q | \-h | \-\-version ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B c5xlnk
combines one or more TMS320C5x TI COFF relocatable objects (as produced by
.BR c5xasm (1))
into a single output. It places the input sections at absolute addresses,
resolves symbol references \-\- including undefined externals matched by name
across objects and pulled from object libraries \-\- applies the relocations
each object records, and writes the linked result.
.PP
By default the output is an absolute, executable COFF
.I .out
file. The default memory map mirrors the usual 'C5x layout: program page
.RB ( PAGE\ 0 )
starts at address
.BR 0x0800 ,
holding
.B .text
immediately followed by
.BR .data ;
data page
.RB ( PAGE\ 1 )
starts at
.B 0x0800
and holds
.BR .bss .
These origins can be overridden (see
.BR \-text ", " \-data ", " \-bss ).
A linker command file given on the command line is detected automatically (it
is any input that is not itself a COFF object) and can describe a richer
.BR MEMORY / SECTIONS
placement.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.IR object .obj ...
One or more relocatable COFF objects to link. At least one input is required.
Inputs are processed in the order given, which also fixes the order in which
like-named sections are concatenated.
.TP
.I cmdfile
An optional linker command file. Any command-line input that is not a COFF
object is treated as a command file and parsed for memory regions, section
placement, symbol assignments and additional object names.
.TP
.BI \-o " file"
Write the linked output to
.IR file .
The default is
.BR a.out .
This selects only the destination.
.TP
.BI \-m " file"
Also write a human-readable link map to
.IR file :
the memory configuration, where each section was placed, and the final
addresses of the global symbols. Useful for verifying placement.
.TP
.BI \-e " symbol"
Set the program entry point to
.IR symbol .
The symbol's final address is recorded in the executable header.
.TP
.BI \-l " lib"
Search the object library
.I lib
(an archive of objects) to satisfy references that are still undefined after
the ordinary inputs have been processed. Only the members that resolve a
needed symbol are pulled in.
.TP
.BI \-i " dir"
Add
.I dir
to the list of directories searched for libraries named with
.BR \-l .
May be given more than once; directories are searched in order.
.TP
.BI \-u " symbol"
Enter
.I symbol
into the link as an unresolved reference before the inputs are read. This
forces the library member that defines
.I symbol
to be pulled in even when nothing else refers to it yet \-\- the usual way to
seed a library search.
.TP
.B \-x
Re-read the libraries repeatedly until a full pass resolves nothing further.
Use it when libraries contain mutually dependent members whose references
point backward as well as forward.
.TP
.B \-r
Produce a relocatable object instead of an absolute executable: addresses are
left unresolved so the output can be linked again later.
.TP
.B \-ar
Like
.BR \-r ,
but the output is additionally marked executable (relocatable-and-absolute):
a partially linked object that still carries executable attributes.
.TP
.B \-s
Strip the symbol table from the output, producing a smaller image that carries
no symbol names. Do not use it if the result must be linked again or
inspected symbolically.
.TP
.B \-\-static
Emit every defined global symbol as a static (module-local) symbol in the
output symbol table rather than as an external. This hides the names from a
later link without removing them from the table.
.TP
.B \-b
Do not merge symbolic-debug information: clear the corresponding file flag
.RB ( 0x1000 )
in the output header.
.TP
.B \-v0
Write a version-0 COFF file header (20 bytes) instead of the default 22-byte
header. Use it only for a consumer that requires the oldest header revision.
.TP
.B \-flat
Instead of a COFF file, emit a flat textual image: one
.RI ' "PAGE AAAA WWWW" '
line per word, giving the page, the address and the 16-bit value. Handy for
inspection, diffing and feeding simple loaders; it is not a COFF object.
.TP
.BI \-text " addr"
Place the
.B .text
(program) section so that it begins at word address
.IR addr ,
overriding the default
.BR 0x0800 .
.TP
.BI \-data " addr"
Place the
.B .data
section at word address
.IR addr
instead of immediately after
.BR .text .
.TP
.BI \-bss " addr"
Place the
.B .bss
(uninitialised data) section at word address
.I addr
on the data page, overriding the default
.BR 0x0800 .
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-verbose
Report section placement and symbol resolution to standard error. Diagnostic
only.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Reduce the start-up identity banner to a single line.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit successfully.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and identity banner and exit successfully.
.SH EXAMPLES
Link two objects into an executable and write a map:
.PP
.EX
c5xlnk main.obj dsp.obj \-o app.out \-m app.map
.EE
.PP
Link against an object library, searching a directory for it:
.PP
.EX
c5xlnk main.obj \-i ./lib \-l mathrt \-o app.out
.EE
.PP
Relocatably combine objects for a later link, overriding the code origin:
.PP
.EX
c5xlnk a.obj b.obj \-r \-text 0x2000 \-o part.obj
.EE
.PP
Produce a flat image of the linked program for inspection:
.PP
.EX
c5xlnk app.obj \-flat \-o app.img
.EE
.SS Full toolchain flow
The three tools chain together: assemble each source to a COFF object with
.BR c5xasm (1),
link the objects into an image with
.BR c5xlnk (1),
then turn that image into a ROM or programmer format such as Intel-HEX with
.BR c5xhex (1).
.PP
Absolute build (the usual case: fixed load addresses, directly loadable),
ending in a standard Intel-HEX file:
.PP
.EX
c5xasm main.asm \-coff \-o main.obj  # source -> COFF object
c5xasm dsp.asm  \-coff \-o dsp.obj
c5xlnk main.obj dsp.obj \-o app.out  # objects -> absolute .out
c5xhex app.out \-f intel \-o app.hex  # .out -> Intel-HEX image
.EE
.PP
Relocatable build (partially link now, finish the absolute link later):
.PP
.EX
c5xasm a.asm \-coff \-o a.obj
c5xasm b.asm \-coff \-o b.obj
c5xlnk a.obj b.obj \-r \-o part.obj   # relocatable, re-linkable
c5xlnk part.obj \-o app.out          # final absolute link
c5xhex app.out \-f srec \-o app.s19   # S-record alternative
.EE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
A read error, an unresolved symbol, or another link-time failure.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error: no input objects, or an unknown or malformed option.
.SH NOTES
The 'C5x is a Harvard-style DSP with separate program and data address spaces,
addressed in 16-bit words rather than bytes. The linker reflects this: program
code and initialised data are placed on the program page
.RB ( PAGE\ 0 ),
while uninitialised data
.RB ( .bss )
is placed on the data page
.RB ( PAGE\ 1 ).
The default origins exist so a simple program links and runs with no command
file at all; a command file or the
.BR \-text / \-data / \-bss
options take over when a specific memory layout is required.
.PP
An absolute executable (the default) has every address resolved and is ready
to load or to convert into a ROM image with
.BR c5xhex (1).
A relocatable object
.RB ( \-r )
keeps its relocation entries so it can be linked again later; use it to build
a program in stages, or to deliver a partially linked unit that another link
will finish.
.PP
Undefined external references are satisfied by matching their names against the
symbols defined in the other inputs. An object library
.RB ( \-l )
is only scanned for members that resolve a reference still outstanding, so
unused members are never pulled in;
.B \-u
seeds that process with an artificial reference, and
.B \-x
repeats the scan until nothing more resolves, which is what makes mutually
dependent library members link.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR c5xasm (1),
.BR c5xhex (1),
.BR c5xdis (1)
